        <p>Wakes up the deep core right before kickoff. Fits between getting changed and the team warm-up. <strong>Same routine works pre-tournament as well</strong> — used it last weekend, will use it again. Three exercises, sixty seconds each, brace and breathe.</p>
        <div class="module-routine">
          <h5>Routine — 3 min · pre-match</h5>
          <div class="move"><div class="name">Dead Bug<span class="desc">slow, focused breathing, brace each rep</span></div><div class="scheme">8 reps/side</div></div>
          <div class="move"><div class="name">Bird Dog<span class="desc">2 sec hold at extension</span></div><div class="scheme">6 reps/side</div></div>
          <div class="move"><div class="name">Glute Bridge with Brace<span class="desc">squeeze glutes, brace abs, hold 3 sec at top</span></div><div class="scheme">10 reps</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="module-block core">
      <div class="head" onclick="toggleModule(this)" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-expanded="true"><div class="dot"></div><h3>Core Rules — Read Before Adding</h3><div class="when">Cumulative effect, not single sessions</div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p><strong>Quality over quantity.</strong> A clean Pallof press with proper bracing beats 50 sloppy crunches. If form breaks, the set is done.</p>
        <p><strong>Slow is harder than fast.</strong> Especially Russian twists and rotations — speed reps look impressive, slow reps build the actual stability and strength soccer demands.</p>
        <p><strong>The ribs-tucked rule.</strong> If you ever feel core work in your lower back rather than your abs, your ribs are flared. Tuck them, post-tilt the pelvis, brace through the core. Re-set and continue.</p>
        <p><strong>Both sides equal.</strong> For unilateral work (carries, side planks, Pallof, chops), train both directions identically. As a two-footed player your right-side and left-side rotational power should match — most players are 30-40% asymmetric. You shouldn't be.</p>
        <p><strong>Don't stack two heavy core sessions on one day.</strong> Wednesday's anti-rotation block is heavy on its own — don't add the Friday lateral chain work to Wednesday too. Spread the stimulus across the week.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
